{
  "scene": {
    "id": "check-pilot3",
    "language": "Okay. So thinking about the check situation, which is where, um, you know, your teammate has the ball either on the halfway line, either bringing the ball out of defense, um, and, you know, you're being marked by an opponent. Um, you're trying to move into space to create a passing option for them, uh, to sort of open up the field. Um, and what you do with that passing option is all down to, um, what the opponent following you does. You're going to make decisions relative to how they follow you. They can either follow you really tightly, um, w- in which case, um, you know, you could maybe shake them. Uh, they could follow you at a medium distance, in which case maybe the best option is to play it safe and give the ball back to your teammate. Or they could, um, follow you really, really far away, you know, giving you lo- loads of time and space. And in that situation, that's where you would turn and face the goal to sort of give yourself a whole lot more options and maybe start an attack. So, um, I'm gonna start the recording now, um, and see what happens in this situation and sort of make a decision based on where my opponent is to me. So I press pass. [0]My teammate's going to give me the ball. I'm going to look at where my opponent is. He's[1] sort of close to me. [2]Um, the ball is coming. I can see him right behind me. There's sort of a medium distance between me and him. Um, it's sort of too close, where I wouldn't be able to turn in a complete 360, um, but sort of too far when, um, if I try to, you know, um, lose my opponent, he might be able to, you know, read my movements. So I'm gonna just receive the ball and[3] play a very, very safe pass back to my- my [4]teammate there. And [5]that sort of keeps the ball, gives my teammate a new option. This player has been moved out of position, so, [6]um, suddenly we have a lot more attacking angles to move forward, either from the halfway line or from defense. Um, so yeah. (laughs) Thank you. Thank you.",
